<p style="text-align: center;"><a href="http://civgene.matthewnewhall.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/10/Vulcan.jpg"><img class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-163" alt="Vulcan" src="http://civgene.matthewnewhall.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/10/Vulcan-300x169.jpg" width="300" height="169" /></a></p>
<p>Found a link up on hare.org.  It&#8217;s a reprint of a magazine article.  If you want to read an article instead of a whole book to get a general idea of Robert Hare&#8217;s work, this is a good one to read.</p>
<p><a title="http://www.hare.org/links/saturday.html" href="http://www.hare.org/links/saturday.html">http://www.hare.org/links/saturday.html</a></p>
<p>Among many things it talks about the shelved reform plan for Canadian inmates.  Seems very much like the core idea I had for a code.  Using selfish logic instead of emotional mapping for therapy sessions.</p>
<p>The problem of course is that you are intentionally helping them progress into a sociopath, but it will be effective simply because you are helping them leave the ego, aka:ASPD, aka:violent stage.  But compared to teaching them how to better manipulate empaths as &#8216;progress&#8217; in group therapy, it&#8217;s a major improvement.</p>
<p>Interestingly this may be where Jeff Lindsay came up with Dexter.  Not a big leap to a &#8216;selfish&#8217; code from Hare&#8217;s prison reform system.  I also think Gene Roddenberry may have stumbled(?) onto a similar answer and expressed it with the Vulcan&#8217;s near self destruction and subsequent ruthless dedication to logic over impulse in Star Trek.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
